  "prediction": "This report provides an overview of cybersecurity-related legislation and hearings in the United States Congress from 2017 to the present. The report highlights five significant pieces of legislation that have been enacted into law, including the National Defense Authorization Acts for Fiscal Years 2018 and 2019, which established various cybersecurity efforts and programs. The report also mentions the FITARA Enhancement Act of 2017, which requires risk management reviews of high-risk IT investments, and the Strengthening State and Local Cyber Crime Fighting Act of 2017, which authorized a National Computer Forensics Institute within the U.S. Secret Service.\n\nThe report also lists numerous cybersecurity-related hearings held in the House and Senate from 2017 to the present. The hearings covered a range of topics, including cybersecurity challenges, information sharing, and the role of federal laws in cybersecurity. The report notes that the list of hearings is not definitive, as no single objective selection criterion was used to identify which hearings to include.\n\nThe report provides links to various CRS reports and publications that provide more detailed information on cybersecurity issues and legislation. Overall, the report serves as a useful resource for those interested in understanding the legislative response to cybersecurity challenges in the United States.",
  "scored_prediction": "This report provides an overview of cybersecurity-related legislation and hearings in the United States Congress from 2017 to the present. The report highlights five significant pieces of legislation that have been enacted into law, including the National Defense Authorization Acts for Fiscal Years 2018 and 2019, which established various cybersecurity efforts and programs. The report also mentions the FITARA Enhancement Act of 2017, which requires risk management reviews of high-risk IT investments, and the Strengthening State and Local Cyber Crime Fighting Act of 2017, which authorized a National Computer Forensics Institute within the U.S. Secret Service.\n\nThe report also lists numerous cybersecurity-related hearings held in the House and Senate from 2017 to the present. The hearings covered a range of topics, including cybersecurity challenges, information sharing, and the role of federal laws in cybersecurity. The report notes that the list of hearings is not definitive, as no single objective selection criterion was used to identify which hearings to include.\n\nThe report provides links to various CRS reports and publications that provide more detailed information on cybersecurity issues and legislation. Overall, the report serves as a useful resource for those interested in understanding the legislative response to cybersecurity challenges in the United States.",
  "score": 17.34693830804874,
  "answers": [
    "Most major cybersecurity legislative provisions were enacted prior to 2002, despite many recommendations having been made over the past decade. More recently, in the 115th and 116th Congresses, cybersecurity legislation has received either committee or floor action or final passage, and both chambers have held multiple hearings. In the 116th Congress, a number of House and Senate bills have received consideration, and hearings have been held by committees in each chamber. In the 115th Congress, 31 bills received some type of action (committee consideration or passage by one or both chambers). Five bills became public law. The House held 54 hearings on cybersecurity issues and the Senate held 40 hearings."
  ],
